course: dictionary
lesson: 7146
title: Perceive: to notice or to understand
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Perceive is a formal word. In everyday talk we say «notice» or «see», but in books, exams and science you will meet perceive. To perceive something is to become aware of it through your senses, like your eyes, ears or nose. Light is perceived by the eye.
explain: The second meaning is about understanding or judging something in a certain way. «She was perceived as shy» means other people thought she was shy. Look at the words around it: perceived as tells you that this is about opinion, not seeing.
explain: Badger uses it in a famous story: «Mole, I perceive you have more sense in your little finger than some other animals have in the whole of their fat bodies.» Here perceive means notice or realise. Swap in notice and the sentence still makes sense, so the check works.
fact: Perceive means to notice or become aware of something through your senses.
fact: Perceive can also mean to understand or think of someone or something in a certain way.
fact: Perception is the noun: the way you notice or understand things.
fact: Perceptive is the adjective: someone who is good at noticing things.
fact: Perceive is formal, so in everyday writing you can often swap it for «notice» or «see».
exercise: Badger says «I perceive you have more sense than other animals.» What does perceive mean here? | *Notice or realise | Forget | Hide from | Dance around
exercise: «Light is perceived by the eye.» What does this tell you? | The eye makes light | *The eye notices light through the senses | Light is hidden from the eye | Light is too heavy to lift
exercise: Perceive is a verb. Which word is its noun? | perceived | *perception | perceptible | perceptive
test: «She was perceived as shy by her class.» What does perceived mean here? | Hidden from view | Loved very much | *Thought of as, in a certain way | Seen clearly in the dark
test: Which word describes someone who is good at noticing things? | perceive | perception | perceived | *perceptive
exam: Ruby says perceive only means to see with your eyes. Is she right? | No, it means to be very tired | *No, it can also mean to understand something in a certain way | Yes, it means to forget | Yes, it only means to hear something
